---
import PersonalBaseLayout from '../layouts/PersonalBaseLayout.astro';

const pageTitle = "Uses";
---
<PersonalBaseLayout>
<h1>Tech</h1>
<ul>
    <li>Velcro Cable Ties</li>
    <p> I prefer these over traditional cable ties as they can be removed 
        more easily. This means if you prefer to figure out cable grouping as you go rather than 
        planning it out beforehand, these might be a good pick.</p>
    <li>Sony MDR-7506</li>
    <p> These are some relatively cheap ($100 without a sale) headphones that are made well and, 
        if my friend's experience is any indication, will last.</p>
    <li>USB DVD Player</li>
    <p> Want to be able to access your music, movies, and shows until the end of time? One of these 
        will connect to your computer to not only let you watch off the disk, but also rip the 
        stuff you bought to store digitally in a media server for example.
    </p>
</ul>
<h2>Web Dev</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Astro</li>
    <p> It's my static site generator. It has a little tutorial on how to make a blog 
        (complete with RSS feed) and lets you write pages in markdown instead of just html. This 
        means that I can plan upgrading the look of my blog posts in something like obsidian for 
        the sake of a trial run.</p>
    <li>Netlify</li>
    <p> I use this to update my site every time I make a push in Git.</p>
    <li>VSCode/Codium</li>
    <p> VSCode is lightweight and has great C language support. VSCodium is a fork that gives most 
        of the same features but is Microsoft free so no AI but also a lot of the C language 
        support is gone, hence why I use both.
    </p>
</ul>
<h2>Gaming</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Nintendo Switch</li>
    <p> The 1st one so no Pokopia but it'll play Rhythm Heaven Groove so I'm not too worried.</p>
    <li>Panic Playdate</li>
    <p> I want to make a little shrine to this thing as I've had such good experiences with it. 
        Basically it's a small yellow handheld (literally as it's a little bigger than my palm) 
        with a 1 bit screen and a small crank along with a dpad and an A and B button. It's kinda
        expensive but as someone who waited on purchasing one for a while, the community and the 
        things I've seen from them as well as how they show up for and support each other made my
        mind up. That and the idea of doing some console development on something that doesn't 
        require an approval process haha.</p>
    <li>Galaxy New! 3DSXL</li>
    <p> I've had this since I was a kid and love it to pieces.</p>
    <li>PS4</li>
    <p> This was actually my first no-Nintendo console that I got during college.</p>
    <li>WiiU</li>
    <p> Also from my childhood. Nice to know my family was one of the 5 that actually bought a 
        WiiU.</p>
</ul>
<h2>Assorted Cool Apps and Programs</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Jellyfin</li>
    <p> This is what I use for my media server. It is completely free but gave me a little bit of 
        trouble the first time I tried to set it up. Once you get it going it's pretty easy though.</p>
    <li>Symphonium</li>
    <p> A cheap music player app that I use for playing music from my server on my phone. Seems 
        pretty customizable although I haven't fully explored that part yet.</p>
    <li>Obsidian</li>
    <p> A notetaking app that's highly customizable and able to be used for ideating, brainstorming
        writing, game dev, normal notes, commonplacing... It's a great free tool that works on all
        major operating systems plus Apple and Android phones.</p>
    <li>CSPenguin</li>
    <p> Some code that makes setting up Clip Studio Paint on Linux super easy. This is not a way
        to pirate Clip, just a way to use the software you already use on your preferred OS. Works
        with lots of distros too!
    </p>
    <li>Asunder</li>
    <p> I <i>think</i> this is only for Linux but it's great for ripping CDs.</p>
    <li>Tailscale</li>
    <p> A VPN that I use for connecting to my media server while out of the house.</p>
</ul>

<h1>Art</h1>
<h2>Game Dev</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Unity</li>
    <p> They can make some <i>interesting</i> business decisions but it's the engine I have the
    most experience with as it's the main one I used in college.</p>
    <li>Unreal Engine 5</li>
    <p> Also makes <i>interesting</i> business decisions but I do like the engine. Once I got my
    head out of my ass, blueprints actually made my life easier. Do not fear them.</p>
    <li>Naninovel</li>
    <p> I love this software so much I did a talk on it. It's an asset for Unity that assists
        in the making of story-based games, particularly visual novels. The price is a bit steep
        (even though it's worth every penny) so look out for it on sale.
    </p>
    <li>Godot</li>
    <p> It's free, it's lightweight, it's getting better all the time, it's also anti-AI. What's
        not to love.</p>
    <li>Playdate Simulator</li>
    <p> This is what you can use to playtest Playdate games without the console. It works well.</p>
    <li>Reaper</li>
    <p> The DAW I use. It's real scary looking but I'm slowly getting used to it. I also appreciate
        that it's afforable.
    </p>
    <li>Itch.io</li>
    <p> My favorite game platform and the only one I've used as a developer. The fact that anyone 
        can post their comic/game/asset there for free is crazy. It's less of a free now
        thanks to the payment processor crackdown but it's still <i>the</i> place to find indie
        gems.</p>
    <li>WriterSolo</li>
    <p> The non-collaboration based version of WriterDuet. It's one of the few good free options
        for writing scripts in. Opposed to just using Fountain in Obsidian, it auto formats.</p>
</ul>
<h2>Visual Art</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Xencelabs Pen Display 16 Lite</li>
    <p> This is really expensive but I tried a Xencelabs tablet at GDC a few years ago when the 
        brand was just starting out and fell in love. Works great on Windows but may cause 
        trouble with Wayland if you're on Linux and have another monitor.</p>
    <li>Tourbox Elite</li>
    <p> An ergonomic alternative to shortcut remote that you don't have to constantly look down at.
        Every button can be distinguished by feel and position and you can toggle an overlay that
        says what each button does.</p>
    <li>Clip Studio Paint</li>
    <p> The only subscription I genuinely want to keep forever. I love this software. Hate 
        animating in it though.</p>
    <li>Aseprite</li>
    <p> In my opinion, the best pixel art making program out there. I love it for tilesets but
        most often use it for character based illustrations and animations.
    </p>
    <li>Blender</li>
    <p> Looks intimidating as hell at first but it is genuinely crazy how much it can do and how 
        well it does it while staying free.</p>
    <li>PureRef</li>
    <p> A software that allows you to have a little window where you can keep all of your art
        references. You can also draw and make notes on the images.</p>
</ul>
<h1>Stationery and Misc Stuff</h1>
<ul>
    <li>Hobonichi Cousin</li>
    <p> Less of a rec if they stick with the decision to force US buyers to get a cover with their
        planners again. But the paper is really nice while still being very thin (tip: if you're 
        used to thicker paper and worried because you can read what you wrote on the other side, 
        9 times out of 10, you're seeing ghosting <i>not</i> bleedthrough.)
    </p>
    <li>Pentel EnerGel</li>
    <p> Really vibrant well formulated ink.</p>
    <li>Mildliners</li>
    <p> No bleedthrough even on very thin paper and comes in a large selection of colors.</p>
    <li>Transparent Sticky Notes</li>
    <p> I HATE writing in books so when I want to annotate I use these. When they're flat, you 
        can't even tell they're there. Mine don't play nice with every type of pen though so stay'</p>
        vigilant.
    <li>Kobo Libra Colour</li>
    <p> Great ereader that's not a kindle. I still have my decade old kindle but I can't recommend
        those after Amazon's recent actions. These are also easy to sideload books onto. They also
        come with an Instapaper app on it so you can access stuff you saved using that.</p>
    <li>Physical TImers</li>
</ul>
<h1>Used To Use/Not Often Used But Recommended</h1>
<ul>
    <li>LightVN</li>
    <p> Still learning this one, but it has a long development history and a really responsive
        dev who answers questions. It also is very easy to create non-VNs in it with making a 
        walkable area super easy. I plan to use this one for a particular game but until then it'll
        remain in this category.
    </p>
    <li>RPGMaker</li>
    <p> Technically doing some prototyping in this so it could go on the other list, but it isn't
        a go to anymore. It can be limiting but those limitations make it easy to just use to 
        prototype. This entry is for the entire series although I've only used VX Ace, MV, and MZ 
        really as a lot of the core is the same. These go on sale constantly and if even 10%
        of the current forum posts get archived there's a veritable wealth of knowledge out there.</p>
    <li>Musescore</li>
    <p> What used to be my go to music making software. When I was younger, typical DAWs were really
         scary and confusing but I still wanted to make music and could read sheet music.</p>
    <li>XPPen Artist 16</li>
    <p> My first screen tablet and the only other one I've used besides my Xencelabs. It stayed 
        working perfectly for 5+ years and I got it on sale. (XPPen has them frequently) If I 
        hadn't absolutely clicked with the Xencelabs tablet, I'd still be using that one. Also that
        one worked on every Linux distro I've used perfectly.</p>
</ul>
</PersonalBaseLayout>